comments.body:
The change looks right to me, and the diagnosis matches what I see in the code: `setIntf()` creates the RIF with `port.m_mac` when the port has one, while the update path fell back to `gMacAddress`, so a VLAN carrying its own MAC gets a spurious `SET SRC_MAC` immediately after create.

One note on scope. Both sides of that asymmetry arrived together in f0c53b94 (#4615), which also added the SAG support. `m_sagMac` is only used to derive the link-local EUI64 route and never sets the RIF `SRC_MAC`, so a static anycast gateway MAC reaches the RIF through `VLAN_TABLE` and `port.m_mac` in exactly the same way the dual-ToR shared MAC does. That suggests this is not specific to dual-ToR: an anycast gateway VLAN would get overwritten the same way, so the fix likely covers more than the title implies.

@pbrisset one question on intent, since it decides which shape of fix is correct here. Is the `if (!mac)` branch in the update path meant to actively re-assert a default MAC onto the RIF, or is it only there to give the comparison a value? If nothing is configured in `INTF_TABLE.mac_addr`, there is arguably nothing to reconcile, and returning early would avoid carrying two copies of the "which MAC do we default to" rule that have to stay in agreement. If it is meant to re-assert, then it probably wants to share a single helper with `setIntf()` rather than repeat the expression in both places.
